# Rebuild the evidence page as readable prose with unambiguous scope and reproducible links.
style='body{margin:0;background:#031326;color:#c7dce9;font:17px/1.8 system-ui}main{max-width:840px;margin:auto;padding:45px 24px}h1,h2{font-family:Georgia;color:#f0c17e;line-height:1.15}h1{font-size:clamp(35px,6vw,58px)}h2{margin-top:40px}a{color:#65dfe8}strong{color:#edf8fa}code{font-size:13px;overflow-wrap:anywhere}.scope{padding:20px;border:1px solid #40dfe844;border-radius:10px;background:#082538}li{margin:8px 0}'
proof='''<a href="/">← Read the RiverRoo story</a><p>Evidence page · Version 1.5.0 · October 23, 2026</p><h1>A feedback-selected setting, tested on unseen full hands.</h1><p class="scope">This test compares two configurations of a new full-hand heuristic against one seeded novice opponent. It does not compare the original uninformed learner with a continuously trained successor. It does not isolate a performance benefit caused by RuVector.</p><h2>What was tested?</h2><p>The experiment ran on RuOS at 1:48 PM Eastern on October 23, 2026. It used a heads-up, play-money no-limit Hold’em engine covering preflop, flop, turn and river, with fresh 100-big-blind stacks for each hand.</p><p>A heuristic makes decisions using sampled equity and pot odds. Five predefined configurations adjust aggression threshold, call margin and bet size. Each configuration played 64 training seeds from both seats: 128 hands per setting, 640 training hands in total. The highest-mean setting was selected and frozen before testing.</p><p>The frozen default and selected setting were then tested on 256 separate seeds from both seats: 512 hands per policy, or 1,024 holdout hands in total. Training and test seeds do not overlap. Seeds make the runs reproducible; playing from both seats swaps the positions.</p><h2>What changed?</h2><p>The default averaged <strong>4.64746 big blinds per hand</strong>. The selected configuration averaged <strong>7.46777</strong>. The paired difference was <strong>+2.82031</strong>, with a normal-approximation 95% interval of <strong>[+1.14400, +4.49662]</strong>, calculated across the 256 paired seed clusters.</p><p>A big blind is the compulsory starting stake. Here it is a simulated-chip unit, not money. Higher average profit is better. The interval describes sampling uncertainty in this single test setup; it does not establish strength against other opponents.</p><p>An independent Python calculation reproduced the training selection, test means, paired difference, interval, and train/test separation from the saved raw results.</p><h2>What learned, and what did RVF do?</h2><p>The explicit feedback-selection code chose aggression threshold 0.60, call margin 0.12 and pot fraction 0.90. This is bounded offline parameter calibration, not a neural self-play learner or mastery of the ten poker abilities.</p><p>The native RVF database accepted 641 vectors: 640 training experiences and a selected-policy marker. It was closed and reopened. The exact marker <code>selected-policy-3</code> was retrieved at distance zero; the corresponding configuration was used in an actual engine decision. A second independent command reopened the file on RuOS and reproduced the lookup.</p><p>RVF persists and retrieves the experience and policy marker. The selection code performs the optimisation. A conventional lookup of the same configuration could produce the same action. An independent performance benefit from vector retrieval has not been measured.</p><h2>Limits that matter</h2><ul><li>One seeded novice opponent, rather than a field of competitive players.</li><li>Five fixed candidate settings, 60 equity rollouts per decision, and fresh stacks each hand.</li><li>One training selection and one holdout set; no independent training replicates.</li><li>No claim of GTO play, tournament ability, live opponent adaptation, or improvement across all ten attributes.</li><li>The playable browser demo still uses its default configuration. It does not load this experiment’s selected setting.</li><li>Adapting rUv’s poker-darwin into the running full-game policy remains unfinished.</li></ul><h2>Download the receipts</h2><ul><li><a href="new-learning-proof.mjs">Complete experiment source</a></li><li><a href="new-learning-proof-preregister.json">Preregistered seed sets, candidates and selection rule</a></li><li><a href="new-learning-proof-selection.json">Frozen selection</a></li><li><a href="new-learning-proof-receipt.json">All raw training and holdout outcomes</a></li><li><a href="learning-independent-recompute.json">Independent arithmetic check</a></li><li><a href="new-learning-proof-experience.rvf">RVF binary</a></li></ul><p>The source requires the accompanying engine and strategy files and package dependencies in <a href="https://github.com/stuinfla/riverroo-poker-story/tree/main/source/fullbot">the repository’s source/fullbot directory</a>. Saved experiment filenames are protected against accidental overwrite; use a clean copy and a distinct output prefix for a new run.</p>'''
Path('public/evidence/learning-proof.html').write_text('<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>RiverRoo — Full-hand feedback experiment</title><style>'+style+'</style></head><body><main>'+proof+'</main></body></html>')
# Update the summary report without silently replacing historical experiments.
report='''<a href="/">← Open the self-guided visual story</a><p>RiverRoo · Report version 1.5.0 · October 23, 2026</p><h1>What we built, what changed, and what remains.</h1><div class="scope"><strong>Current state:</strong> a playable full-street prototype; a recovered ten-ability framework; verified memory storage and retrieval; and one bounded offline parameter-calibration improvement against a novice opponent. A broadly strong trained poker player and integrated poker-darwin policy remain unfinished.</div><h2>Three experiments, not one continuous training history</h2><p>The early simplified learner, the restricted reference-memory comparison, and the newer full-hand parameter test are separate demonstrations. Their scores use different games, opponents and reward definitions. Do not place them on one learning curve or subtract their results as if they measured the same thing.</p><h2>The ten-ability framework</h2><p>Mathematics; game theory; bluffing; opponent psychology; deception; position; tactical opportunism; adaptation; tournament intelligence; metacognition.</p><p>These names were recovered from the earlier project’s build prompt and 10 AM checkpoint. No numerical ten-attribute baseline was recorded. Stuart describes their origin as research organised through Ruflo; the original completed research output was not recovered. The story credits that account and exposes the provenance boundary.</p><h2>The newer full-hand result</h2><p>The frozen default heuristic averaged 4.65 simulated big blinds per hand; a feedback-selected setting averaged 7.47 against one novice opponent. The paired gain was +2.82, with an approximate 95% interval from +1.14 to +4.50 across 256 unseen seed clusters. Five configurations were compared on 640 training hands before selection. Each test policy played 512 holdout hands.</p><p>This is offline parameter selection, not proof of continual learning, expert strength or ten-dimensional improvement. <a href="/evidence/learning-proof.html">Read the complete method, limits and raw receipts</a>.</p><h2>The tools’ roles</h2><ul><li><strong>RuOS:</strong> remote environment where the engine and experiments executed.</li><li><strong>Ruflo:</strong> work coordination and the research-workflow framing. Tracked tasks alone do not prove autonomous research completion.</li><li><strong>RuvNet Brain:</strong> source research that located existing poker implementations.</li><li><strong>rUv’s poker-darwin and local-logic agents:</strong> discovered foundations for investigation; integration is unfinished.</li><li><strong>RuVector / RVF:</strong> reference retrieval and native persistence. The new RVF file accepted 641 vectors, reopened successfully, and supplied the selected-policy marker for an actual decision. Its independent contribution to performance has not been isolated.</li></ul><h2>Technical appendix: earlier restricted comparison</h2><p>The corrected elementary reference-case experiment used 16,000 paired deals in a 10-big-blind preflop shove/fold environment. Its case-minus-uniform difference was +0.02375 big blinds per hand, with an approximate 95% interval of +0.00960 to +0.03790. Direct and vector retrieval made identical decisions. This is not full poker or the new full-hand learning result.</p><p><a href="/evidence/repair-note.md">Corrected source report</a> · <a href="/evidence/REPORT.md">Preserved report before correction</a></p><h2>What comes next</h2><p>Adapt and test the relevant rUv poker implementation; qualify against stronger and varied opponents; isolate memory’s effect; connect a validated setting to the playable app; and create separate tests for the ten abilities. Preserve the baseline and promote changes only after evaluation on unseen hands.</p><p><a href="/play/">Play the default full-street prototype</a> · <a href="/RiverRoo-Presentation.zip">Download the story backup</a></p>'''
Path('public/final-report.html').write_text('<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>RiverRoo — Results and completion report</title><meta property="og:image" content="https://riverroo-poker-story.vercel.app/social-card-v1.3.png"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><style>'+style+'</style></head><body><main>'+report+'</main></body></html>')
Path('README.md').write_text('''# RiverRoo — the self-guided RuvNet learning story

Version 1.5.0. Permanent public URL: https://riverroo-poker-story.vercel.app/

The eight chapters introduce the beginner, research question and ten-ability framework; the full-hand prototype; RuvNet Brain discovery of rUv poker implementations; memory versus learning; a bounded feedback-selection result; and the next tests. The story defines its tools, units, provenance and limitations without a presenter.

The new full-hand experiment is separate from the original learner and earlier restricted memory test. It demonstrates offline parameter calibration against one novice opponent, not expert poker or a causal advantage from vector retrieval. The playable UI uses its default setting. Poker-darwin integration remains unfinished.

Receipts: public/evidence/learning-proof.html. Full experiment source and dependencies: source/fullbot/. The preregistration and result files are intentionally write-protected against accidental replacement; use a new output prefix for any new run.
''')
